xnu 0197: the door thread must not write to the log

ring32 shows the thread working -- 2000 bring-up doors plus the first client
submission, every one "uc door posted" -- and the system dying right after the
first client door. The thread flushed the disk on every door: 2000 UFS writes,
each holding mondrian_ufs_lock across a bounce-buffer write and a cache flush,
from a thread that is neither the log thread nor the UFS write path the logger
was written for. The console ring then cannot be flushed either (try_lock) and
the reset thread blocks in its BCB write, so the boot ends with no further
lines.

The door thread only touches the register now. The submission logs the pair
around it, from the caller: "uc door <seq> at <dword>" before waking the thread
and "uc door posted <seq>" once the memory flag says the write was issued, both
only for streams from a real driver. The log has one writer again.

--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
@@ -3044,13 +3044,9 @@
 			continue;
 		}
 		mondrian_uc_door_at = 0;
-		printf("MONDRIAN GPU: uc door %u at 0x%x\n", seq, at);
-		mondrian_log_flush_now();
 		mondrian_gpu_write(mondrian_uc_gpu, GEN7_CP_RB_WPTR * 4u, at);
 		__asm__ volatile("dsb sy" ::: "memory");
 		mondrian_uc_door_posted = seq;
-		printf("MONDRIAN GPU: uc door posted %u\n", seq);
-		mondrian_log_flush_now();
 	}
 }
 
@@ -3130,6 +3126,10 @@
 	if (mondrian_uc_async != 0 && mondrian_uc_door_started != 0) {
 		uint32_t waited;
 
+		if (dwords > 16) {
+			printf("MONDRIAN GPU: uc door %u at 0x%x\n", seq, at);
+			mondrian_log_flush_now();
+		}
 		mondrian_uc_door_seq = seq;
 		mondrian_uc_door_at = at;
 		wakeup(&mondrian_uc_door_at);
@@ -3147,6 +3147,10 @@
 			mondrian_uc_up = 0;
 			return 0;
 		}
+		if (dwords > 16) {
+			printf("MONDRIAN GPU: uc door posted %u\n", seq);
+			mondrian_log_flush_now();
+		}
 	} else {
 		mondrian_gpu_write(mondrian_uc_gpu, GEN7_CP_RB_WPTR * 4u, at);
 	}
